  "defaults": {
    "slides": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "title": "Introduction",
        "content": "Today: higher-degree equations and polynomials as modeling tools.\nKey questions: how to find roots and factor polynomials.",
        "notes": "State the workflow clearly: candidates → check → factorization."
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "title": "Lesson goal",
        "content": "• Understand the rational root algorithm.\n• Use Horner's method.\n• Divide and factor polynomials.",
        "notes": "Focus on verification and structuring the solution."
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "title": "Rational roots",
        "content": "If a root is rational, it has the form $p/q$.\nHere, $p$ divides the constant term and $q$ divides the leading coefficient.",
        "notes": "Show how to build the candidate list quickly."
      },
      {
        "id": 4,
        "title": "Horner's method",
        "content": "Horner's method is a fast way to test a root and divide by $(x-a)$.\nIt gives the remainder and quotient coefficients.",
        "notes": "Make clear that the remainder equals the polynomial value at a."
      },
      {
        "id": 5,
        "title": "Polynomial division",
        "content": "Long division: divide leading terms, subtract, repeat.\nResult: quotient + remainder.",
        "notes": "Show an example on the board and connect it to Horner's method."
      },
      {
        "id": 6,
        "title": "Worked example",
        "content": "Solve: $x^3 - 4x^2 - x + 4 = 0$.\nCandidates: ±1, ±2, ±4. For example, test $x=2$.",
        "notes": "Work through Horner's method with the class, then factor."
      },
      {
        "id": 7,
        "title": "Practice",
        "content": "Move to the task cards. Choose a problem and record an attempt.\nUse hints, checking, and the full solution.",
        "notes": "Emphasize an independent attempt first."
      },
      {
        "id": 8,
        "title": "Summary",
        "content": "Algorithm: candidates → check → factorization → solution.\nHomework: 2–3 cards from the list.",
        "notes": "Summarize what the class handled best."
      }
    ]
  }
